  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "The set plausibly supports reconstruction of the central study. R3 already applies high-strain-rate, temperature-dependent MD tension to a closely related FCC HEA, while R7 applies MD deformation to CoNiCrFeMn nanowires and explicitly identifies hindered partial-dislocation motion, FCC-to-HCP-like structural evolution, and temperature-dependent behavior. R1, R4, and R5 further connect tensile MD, temperature, partial-dislocation nucleation, and stacking faults, and R2 supplies bulk Cantor-alloy temperature-dependent tensile behavior. An expert could reasonably combine these teachings to propose orientation-, temperature-, and strain-rate-dependent tensile MD for bulk FeNiCrMn and expect Shockley-partial/stacking-fault-mediated plasticity and thermal softening. The exact crystallographic directions, vacancy-cluster nucleation sites, absence of twinning or bulk transformation, and strain-rate-insensitive yield strength are not directly anticipated, but they are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
